#X text 491 118 The loop~ generates a sawtooth whose frequency is determined by a transposition factor (left inlet) and window size (right inlet)., f 33;
#X text 492 186 The window size is just the size of the grain as a number of samples., f 33;
#X text 490 228 The transposition factor is calculated based on the pitch of the incoming MIDI note., f 33;
#X text 491 271 Using loop~ ensures that if the grain size is changed by the user \, the sound will change smoothly., f 33;
#X text 507 398 This will send a bang when the main sawtooth is about to reach 1, f 30;
#X text 506 440 The randomly selected grain start position and pan are re-generated., f 30;
#X text -40 557 Next add the grain start position \, which is the position in the sample (between 0 and 1) where the snippet begins., f 32;
#X text -38 656 Multiply by the number of samples in the sample., f 34;
#X text -38 411 The next few steps turn the sawtooth into a signal of sample numbers covering the correct interval of the sample. This signal will be used to read from the sample., f 37;
#X text -38 475 First \, the main sawtooth (after perhaps being inverted) is multiplied by (grain_size / sample_size) \, both values measured in samples., f 37;
#X text -37 785 Read from the sample.;
